{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-108127",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "At $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x) & =g(2)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(2+a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(x-1) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(2+a) & =1 \\\\ 2+a & ={10}^{1} \\\\ 2+a & =10 \\\\ a & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
